What is driving demand for battery foils?
Growth in electrode manufacturing, thinner current collectors and the need for reliable material qualification underpin the outlook. Copper generally carries current from the anode coating and aluminum supports cathode coatings. Each must meet distinct surface, mechanical and chemical requirements before a cell maker can use it consistently.

October 2026 copper-foil development targets silicon-rich anodes
On October 20, 2026, SK Nexilis, part of SKC, announced that it had developed a high-adhesion carbon-coated copper foil designed for silicon-containing anodes and dry electrode processes. The company described its coating as a way to improve bonding between active anode material and the collector and said it was preparing a pilot production system. It targeted commercialization in the second half of 2027, rather than claiming full commercial rollout in October.

That distinction is material for market analysis. Silicon can change volume during battery cycling, creating stress at the interface with the conductive substrate. A coating that helps maintain adhesion may improve electrode robustness, but adoption still requires production-line trials, cell testing and customer approval. For foil manufacturers, higher-value surface treatments could become a competitive differentiator while also increasing the cost and complexity of producing a consistent roll.

